Someone can want a better way of doing something and still be reluctant to leave the old one behind. They can understand the value, like the idea, and decide it isn’t for them. What looks inconsistent from the outside often has a perfectly reasonable explanation once you know a little more.

I find that endlessly interesting.

We bring our habits, expectations, doubts, and ambitions into the things we use. Some we can explain. Others only become apparent when something asks us to change. Even then, we might not quite know what’s making us hesitate.

> Understanding what someone wants is part of the work. Understanding what makes it difficult for them is where things get interesting.

## I like understanding how things fit together.

What someone tells you, what they do, and the circumstances around them each reveal something. A conversation might explain a hesitation you noticed. A workaround might tell you more about what matters than the answer to a direct question. Something that seems unnecessary to us might be giving them a sense of control they’re quite right to care about.

You have to spend time with these things. Ask another question. Notice where your explanation stops making sense. Be willing to give up an idea you were rather pleased with.

And you have to look beyond the moment you’re trying to improve.

People arrive with expectations that started forming long before that interaction. Something they read. A conversation with sales. A previous experience that taught them to be careful. All of it comes with them.

**One touchpoint can show you where something breaks. Understanding why usually takes you further.**

## This work can be pure chaos.

The customer sees one part. The business sees another. The people building it know things that neither of them does. There are constraints, competing priorities, and decisions made months ago that everyone has learnt to work around.

I’m oddly at home in that.

I like pulling the pieces apart, hearing how different people understand the same situation, and gradually finding the connections. Especially when something small changes how we see the whole problem.

That understanding has to lead somewhere. We might need to explain something differently, ask less of someone, give them more information, or resolve a decision we’ve been passing along to them. What we learn should shape what we make.

> The experience someone has with a business is shaped by far more than the part we happen to be designing.

That’s why my work moves across product, brand, websites, and systems. I care about how those parts influence each other, because people carry what they experience in one into the next. A reassuring promise means very little if the next interaction gives them a reason to doubt it.

## And then, sometimes, it feels obvious.

Something that took a lot of questioning and untangling starts to make sense. People understand what’s possible. They know enough to make a decision. They can get on with what they came to do.

They may never know how much had to change for that to happen. But they feel the difference.

I find a lot of joy in that. All those conversations, loose ends, and difficult decisions becoming an experience someone can take in their stride.
